## Motivation

The paper addresses the challenge of improving the efficiency and performance of masked language models (MLMs) for natural language processing tasks, particularly in generating coherent and coherent-sounding text. While diffusion models have shown success in various domains, such as image generation, video generation, and audio modeling, their application to natural language remains a complex task due to the discrete nature of language. Existing approaches like Masked Language Diffusion (MLD) and Masked Diffusion Language Models (MDLM) have been proposed to address this challenge, but they come with significant limitations. MLD, inspired by image-based masked diffusion, introduces a mask matrix that varies with each forward pass, making it difficult for the model to learn coherent text. MDLM, which removes the mask and treats each token as a binary random variable, can lose coherent information during the denoising process. Additionally, the forward pass in autoregressive models like GPT requires an exponential number of parameters, and standard autoregressive models can suffer from degenerate behavior where the model only learns the 'noise' distribution. These limitations highlight the need for a new approach that can efficiently and effectively learn both coherent and incoherent language without losing information. The paper introduces Coherent Masked Diffusion (CoMD), a novel framework that addresses these challenges by using a fixed mask matrix and a coherent loss term, aiming to improve both inference and training efficiency while maintaining or enhancing model performance.

## Main Idea

The paper presents Coherent Masked Diffusion (CoMD), a novel framework that extends Masked Language Diffusion (MLD) to more efficiently and effectively learn coherent and incoherent language. CoMD introduces three key innovations: 1) a fixed mask matrix that is independent of the masked-out token, 2) a coherent loss term that optimizes the probability of coherent generations without requiring additional samples per training step, and 3) a variable time parameter to guide the coherent probability towards the ground truth coherent probability. These innovations aim to address the limitations of existing MLD implementations and autoregressive models, leading to improved inference and training efficiency and better performance on coherent tasks.

